Operator: "continue there" (investigating whether StepwiseExplorer's
state-hashing could be aliasing distinct states, following up on
"look at the explorer's candidate generation code"). Found the
state-hashing question is a known, pre-existing, documented
limitation (GAP-ARCH-GRID-ONLY-STATE) unlikely to be sp80's specific
issue (sp80 isn't in HIDDEN_STATE_GAME_IDS) -- but investigating
further surfaced something more consequential.

The REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-7/8/9/10 "explorer frontier exhausts at ~25
transitions regardless of budget" finding was measured entirely
through outer_loop_sge_smoke_test.py, which passes
action_prior=generator AND qd_generator=generator where generator =
ActionDiverseLiveGenerator(max_candidates=8) -- a hard 8-candidate
cap forced onto every step. Production's SUBMITTED_QD_GENERATION_
ENABLED=False means qd_generator defaults to None on the real live
path, so _candidates() never overrides rich_action_candidates()'s own
output -- up to 48 salience-sorted candidates per frame (that
function's own docstring documents fixing exactly a naive 12-click
cap once already).

The REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-12 real live-path A/B (same session, same game)
directly confirms the two stacks behave differently:
reset_replay_steps=6 / forward_walk_hit_rate=~0.54 across actions=241
(near-full budget=250) for both arms -- the signature of an explorer
riding one mostly-novel branch for most of the budget, not hitting a
fast repeated exhaustion wall at transition_count=25.

What's unaffected: 5699-12's own headline (SGE vs discriminative
router, identical outcome, SGE ~3.9x slower) ran on the real stack
directly. What needs re-scoping: 5699-7's "budget cannot help" and
5699-8/9/10's "induction is trust-gated by exploration exhaustion"
were established entirely on the narrow-generator harness; whether
the same pattern holds on the real ~48-candidate generator is open,
not yet directly tested. Production's own explorer still never
leveled up sp80 in 250 actions, so the headline null stands on the
real stack too -- but the WHY is now genuinely uncertain, not
explained by the smoke-test's specific mechanism.

Spec: REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-13 with its own scenario + spec-declaration
test. No code changes -- this is a documentation/analysis correction
using already-collected data, not a new experimental run.

